1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/KLYF-2XD
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT POTKA - Jean-Baptiste Billiau dit L’Espérance
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Lesperance was born on June 25, 1775, the son of Marie Françoise Meny, a woman of Wyandotte (Huron) ancestry, whose name appears in multiple church records throughout mid-18th century Detroit. Marie Meny was married only o
2 CONC nce formally—to Jean-Baptiste Billiau dit L’Espérance, a French soldier born in Grenoble, France. Their marriage took place on May 8, 1758, at Ste. Anne de Détroit, where the priest explicitly described her as a “sauvagesse de nat
2 CONC ion huronne,” confirming her Huron origin and Indigenous status.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Affair
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Although Marie was married in the Church, the relationship did not endure. By 1772, she had begun an affair with Charles Morand dit Grimard, a widower in Detroit. Their union was spiritually condemned.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT On January 23, 1774, Father Simple Bocquet took up his iron gall pen, dipped it in the familiar well of walnut-black ink, and wrote solemnly into the parish register. What followed was not a baptism nor a marriage, but a rare and 
2 CONC grievous act: a formal sentence of excommunication—deliberate, final, and public.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT He wrote:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT “We now feel compelled to speak of another matter, even more serious and painful to recount. For over two years, Charles Morand dit Grimard and Marie Françoise Meny, the wife of Jean Billou dit L’Espérance, also called Potoka, hav
2 CONC e lived in an open and notorious state of adultery, all the more scandalous because of their obstinacy and refusal to heed any correction.”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT “During this time, we have spared neither charitable admonitions, nor prayers, nor threats. Every method of gentle persuasion, correction, and spiritual warning has been used to bring them back to their duty. We reminded them of G
2 CONC od’s justice, which is as fearsome in His punishment as He is generous in His mercy for those who repent. We urged them to consider the eternal consequences of their actions, the danger they placed themselves in, and the shame the
2 CONC y brought upon the Christian community.”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT “But they remained deaf to all exhortations. Not only did they refuse to end their illicit relationship, but they even dared to raise children together while she remained married to another. Their behavior has caused confusion and
2 CONC  scandal within the parish, undermining faith and obedience among other members.”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT “Therefore, in light of their persistent defiance, and after exhausting all possible remedies, we are compelled—though with sorrow—to declare them publicly excommunicated. They are henceforth cut off from the communion of the fait
2 CONC hful and shall remain so until they offer public repentance and make full satisfaction for their offense.”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT “Let this serve as a warning to others.”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Father Bocquet missioned nearly 30 years at St. Ann Detroit, during which time Detroit passed from French to British ownership and then to American. An Anglican wedding is even recorded in the Catholic Church records during this p
2 CONC eriod. His pastoral responsibilities extended across the outlying settlements, serving the people of his parish through baptism, confession, and burial. But this was different. This was an attempt to guard the sanctity of marriage
2 CONC  and, with it, the moral foundation of the community.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT This act of formal excommunication severed the couple from the sacramental life of the Church, but they remained together. Their union produced at least three sons—Antoine, Charles, and Pierre—all of whom carried the surname Lespe
2 CONC rance, though they were fathered by Grimard. The name “Potoka,” associated with Charles, likely comes from his mother and perhaps is just a Wyandot Native nickname.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Legacy of a Woman Unafraid
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie Françoise Meny has often been reduced to a scandalous figure in parish records. But her life tells us more. She asserted her right to love whom she chose, despite the legal and religious frameworks that condemned her. She ra
2 CONC ised three sons who would survive the storm and build a town and a church named Sainte Anne where they settled. She challenged patriarchal norms in ways not acknowledged.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Her son Charles Lesperance carried her resilience into the next generation. By 1794, at just 19 years old, Charles appeared before the Land Board of the District of Hesse to secure a title to Lot 152.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles appears as a Catholic baptized at Ste. Anne Detroit, the second oldest Catholic parish in the United States—a son of a sauvagesse de nation huronne—who would swear an oath of allegiance to the British Crown and become a Lo
2 CONC yalist of its empire. With a Loyalist Lot, now imagine that—and he could speak in three languages.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT A Log Home: Family and Kinship
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1800, Charles married Marie Anne Pitre, a French-Canadian woman from another long-established Detroit River family. Together, they raised eleven children, including sons François Olivier, Clement, Albert, and Ulysses.
2 CONT
2 CONT They appear in the 1851 Canadian Census. Directly beside Charles’s family on Lot 152 was a small Indigenous family. The head of the group, Cabagawsaque, was born around 1791, making him a likely contemporary of Charles—possibly ev
2 CONC en a boyhood acquaintance. Others included Cochin, a 25-year-old hunter; Nauthcajike, a 60-year-old widowed woman also listed as a hunter; and Kicknasawway, a six-year-old boy—almost certainly related to the Kicknosway family, sti
2 CONC ll present on Walpole Island today.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Their presence on Charles’s land speaks to more than hospitality. It suggests a network of kinship and trust—a community rooted not in legal boundaries but in relationship. The son of a Wyandot mother had not forgotten what it mea
2 CONC nt to share land.
2 CONT
2 CONT This arrangement—unusual in a colonial system obsessed with property lines—reflected Charles’s maternal inheritance: a Wyandot ethic of land sharing, kinship, and protection. His log home stood not as a barrier to others but as a 
2 CONC place of co-residence and Métis identity.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles Lesperance died in 1860, a respected patriarch and settler whose life spanned three regimes: French, British, and Canadian. He left a town named Tecumseh, after an Indigenous leader, and shared his land so a church named S
2 CONC ainte Anne could be built.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The log house remains. An Historical Museum in the Town of Tecumseh, Ontario today. His children stayed. And the name Lesperance—though borrowed and complex—carried forward a story of blended families, new loyalties, and the quiet
2 CONC  endurance of a people between nations.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT By: Richard Charles Teno
2 CONT
2 CONT
