1 NAME Marie Esther /Girardin/
2 GIVN Marie Esther
2 SURN Girardin
2 _MARNM Girardin
1 SEX F
1 BIRT
2 DATE 8 MAY 1797
2 ADDR
3 CITY Detroit
3 STAE Michigan
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 JAN 1898
2 ADDR
3 CITY Detroit
3 STAE Michigan
3 CTRY United States
1 BURI
2 DATE 8 JAN 1898
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} She weighed only 2 and a 1/ 2pounds at birth.  Her cradle for her first month was a knife box.  She was so feeble that they had to feed her with a feather and her cries were so faint that they couldn't be heard out
2 CONC side her room.  No one expected she would live but ironically she outlived all of her siblings.  At 94 she was able to read, hear and walk around the house and sing.  She was noted for her remarkable energy.  She sang a verse in G
2 CONC erman, another stanza  in French and could make herself understood speaking English.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She has been accomplished in French and English scholar.  She was very proficient in needlework, lace, and embroidery.  Her preceptor in early studies was Father Gabriel Richard.  She received her first communion from Father Richa
2 CONC rd and attended his funeral in 1832.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She remembers seeing the first steamer, Walk-on-the Water and seeing the crowds gather around to greet it.  It was the first such vessel on the Detroit River.  
2 CONT
2 CONT For 34 years she was the housekeeper for Father de Broux of Grosse Pointe.  She also had a strong recollection of the great fire that destroyed Detroit in 1805.  She also remembers seeing the laying of the foundation for St. Anne'
2 CONC s church at the corner of Bates and Congress Streets.  This church served the French Americans for many generations.  The church was erected by Father Richard in 1818.  
2 CONT
2 CONT She spent many months of the year living at Frank Ellair's house on Atwater Street between Chene and Joseph Campau Street.  Notes taken from article printed on June 21, 1891 from the  Detroit Free Press archives
2 CONT
2 CONT She was 4'6" and weighed no more than 89 pounds.  She died at 98 but had the memory of someone 1/3 of that age.  Her eyesight failed in her advanced years but everything else was fine.  She was famous for her charities.  In times 
2 CONC gone by a request for a physician was always accompanied by a request for "Auntie Girardin".  
2 CONT
2 CONT Her father, a baker, came to Detroit from Montreal in the last years of the 18th century.  He had come with the voyagers to Detroit by way of canoe.  He first settled as a farmer and then as a baker.  Esther was born in 1799 in a 
2 CONC one story log cabin at the corner of Beaubien and Jefferson Ave.  Her father was killed in 1805 fighting the fire in Detroit.  He was a volunteer fireman.  The villagers lived in garrison tents until the village could be rebuilt. 
2 CONC  Everyone assisted.    Detroit Free Press Archives    January 9, 1898
2 CONT
2 CONT From Suzanne Sommerville April 3, 2016:  " Esther Girardin's baptism is digitized on: Ancestry: U.S., French Catholic Church Records (Drouin Collection), 1695-1954 
2 CONT  D Détroit, Ste-Anne  1780-1801 Image 593 of 625 [transcriptions], Marie Ester, 22 August 1799, Father Joseph, mother, Josephe Drouillard. Godparents: Louis Ginel and Marie Anne St Georges, father signed. Original Image 294 of 625
2 CONC  has a clear signature of Jacques Girardin. [This file begins with original registers and continues with transcribed (hand copied) versions that are sometimes easier to read]
2 CONT
2 CONT Memoirs like this always have to be treated carefully. Ester would have been 6 in August of 1805, the year of the fire that destroyed the fort of Detroit, in which, she says, her father died. I had never heard of anyone who died i
2 CONC n the 1805 fire, so I checked Gail Moreau-DesHarnais’ Series of Articles Transcribing and Translating the Burial Records in the Detroit River Region through 1805, which does not list a Girardin as deceased and buried just after th
2 CONC e 1805 fire. See page 177 -178 of the article.
2 CONT
2 CONT 11 June 1805, Detroit burned and with it went the church. There were no burials listed until
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean Baptiste Rivard, about 80 years, 23 June 1805, buried in cemetery, the first burial
2 CONT
2 CONT I have to wonder where her father was buried. Nor can this burial be found in Gail's transcriptions for L’Assomption-de-la-Pointe-de-Montréal-du-Detroit for 1805. I am not saying she erred in saying her father died in the fire, ju
2 CONC st wondering. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://habitantheritage.org/.../Ste_Anne_-_Part_VII_1800..."
