1 NAME James Pierre /Drouillard/
2 GIVN James Pierre
2 SURN Drouillard
2 _MARNM Drouillard
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 31 AUG 1839
2 PLAC Gallipolis, Ohio
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 16 OCT 1892
2 PLAC Nashville Davidson County Tennessee, USA
2 ADDR
1 TITL Capt. (USA),
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=7361256
2 CONT
2 CONT http://drouillard.us/captain_james_pierre_drouillard
2 CONT
2 CONT 1839 – 1892
2 CONT
2 CONT James Pierre Drouillard was born in 1839 in the Ohio River town of Gallipolis, Ohio. His mother was Susan Miller Drouillard, and his father Joseph served with Captain Elias Langham's Company in the War of 1812. James’s Grandfather
2 CONC  was the renowned Indian interpreter Pierre Drouillard who fathered the half-breed Indian scout George Drouillard of the Lewis and Clark Expedition.
2 CONT
2 CONT James was the eighth of nine children, and in 1857 as a young man entered the military academy at West Point and graduated in the distinguished class of 1861. This class included George Armstrong Custer (The Little Big Horn).
2 CONT
2 CONT As the Civil War broke loose, the class of 1861 petitioned the Secretary of War to graduate early so that they could be of service. The request was granted and the Class of 1861 was graduated in two waves, forty-five of them in Ma
2 CONC y 1861, and thirty-four more in June. A few left early to join the seceding states. 
2 CONT
2 CONT James Pierre served as aide-de-camp with General Sykes at the first battle of Bull Run and afterwards with General Rosecrans in the Army of the Cumberland. James was a hero at The Battle of Chattanooga.
2 CONT
2 CONT James was in Nashville during the Northern occupation. On September 21, 1864 James Pierre married a wealthy southern belle Mary Florence Kirkman Drouillard who was the daughter of Hugh Kirkman and Eleanora C. Vanleer and granddaug
2 CONC hter of ironmaster Anthony W. Vanleer and Rebecca Brady.  The marriage of a Yankee Officer to a southern lady of high standing sent a shock wave through Nashville society, and none of Mary Florence friends or family attended the w
2 CONC edding.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the war the Drouillards moved to Dickson County to reopen the family furnace. In 1886 the Drouillards returned to Nashville, dividing their time between the city and Europe. Mary Florence was welcomed back into the city's so
2 CONC ciety to become one of its most colorful social queens of the late nineteenth century. She died May 19, 1905, and is buried with her husband in the family plot in Mt. Olivet Cemetery, Nashville. 
