2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE A minor correction to the post. There is no evidence that Antoine Descompt dit Labadie's country wife, Marie, was Pontiac's daughter. See the French Canadian and Native Families Page on the FCHSM website and download the profile o
3 CONC f the family, starting on page 5.  See https://habitantheritage.org/cpage.php?pt=39 and https://habitantheritage.org/upload/files/Create_Subfolders/Native_Americans_and_metis/Daunois_-_Duguay_Families.pdf
2 NOTE “#WhatisitWednesday #FrancoOntarianDay
3 CONT
3 CONT In honour of Franco-Ontarian Day we are highlighting a pocket sundial compass which belonged to one of the first French settlers in the region—Pierre Decomptes dit Labadie.  
3 CONT
3 CONT This object was designed to tell time. The compass aspect of this item is used to determine north. Once north is determined, the shadow produced by the fine string attached to the narrow metal stem in the upright position indicate
3 CONC s the time. This object was made by Roch Blondeau in 1671 in Paris, France. It was brought to the Detroit region by Pierre Descomptes dit Labadie, one of the first to receive a land concession on the Windsor side of the river in 1
3 CONC 749. In a 1905 address to the Essex Historical Society it is mentioned that the sundial compass was in the possession of Daniel Labadie.
3 CONT
3 CONT  There is speculation that Pierre Descomptes dit Labadie's son, Antoine Labadie was married to Marie Mannon Sauteuse, possibly the daughter of Odawa Chief Pontiac.
3 CONT
3 CONT 1959.128-Gift Courtesy of Hiram Walker & Sons Ltd.” Museum Windsor
