2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/75/92/37/35/53444869f9418c9b/img_1541_original.jpg?hash=84013eaa180046b60b846a1799c021598cf286c311f22525971728e10e4aebdd.1796371199
2 FORM jpg
2 NOTE “ One of the most incredible parts of my family story is that my 6th great grandfather, Pierre Descomps dit Labadie (born 1702 in La Rochelle, France), built what was long considered the oldest surviving house in Detroit.
3 CONT
3 CONT He arrived in Detroit in the mid-1740s from Montreal with his wife, Angelique De Lacelle, and their growing family. They were part of the wave of French Canadian settlers who received ribbon farm grants along the Detroit River — l
3 CONC ong, narrow strips of land stretching from the river inland.
3 CONT
3 CONT His farm was located about 2 miles southwest of Fort Detroit, near the Potawatomi village, along what is now Jefferson Ave and 24th Street.
3 CONT
3 CONT Historical records from the 1750s show Pierre cultivating 24 acres, growing wheat, oats, and corn, while raising horses, oxen, cows, hogs, and poultry.
3 CONT
3 CONT The family witnessed major events. The area was near the site of Pontiac's 1763 Siege of Detroit, and during the War of 1812, when Americans re-occupied the city, the house — then occupied by Pierre's son, my 5th great uncle, Pier
3 CONC re — was hit by gunfire, leaving bullet scars on the logs.  
3 CONT
3 CONT The original structure was a classic French-style log cabin, built around 1760. It was later covered in clapboard siding, and in 1830, two distinctive circular wings were added, giving it that unique, almost whimsical look.
3 CONT
3 CONT By the early 20th century, the house was proudly known as Detroit's oldest home. Sadly, the house was demolished around 1910. During excavation of the site, workers reportedly uncovered skeletons, supporting claims that the land h
3 CONC ad long ago been part of a Potawatomi burial ground.” Marek Zman
