2 CONT =======================================================
2 CONT
2 CONT  <From Our French Canadian-Ancestors" by Thomas J. Laforest. vol.26 pg.40-48>
2 CONT
2 CONT  MATHIEU
2 CONT
2 CONT Mathieu Amiot, Sieur de Villeneuve, born in France about 1628, owned a small lot in 1655, measuring 54 feet in frontage on the chemin Saint-Louis, to a depth of 18 feet, which ran as far as the south-western part of the land of Si
2 CONC eur Chartier de Lotbiniére, near Cap-aux-Diamants.
2 CONT
2 CONT On the occasion of his marriage to Marie Miville, on 21 November 1650, at Quebec, Mathieu received from his father-in-law Pierre Miville, a plot of land on the Grande-Allee, border-ing that of Jean Bourdon. Mathieu owned several o
2 CONC ther pieces: at Sillery, at the Chatellenie de Coulonges, at Saint-Augustin and at Cap-Rouge. The census of 1681 recorded the colonist Amiot as living in the seigneurie of Maure, between Tugal Catin and Jacques Lemarie, where he o
2 CONC wned three head of cattle and had thirty arpents of land under cultivation.
2 CONT
2 CONT To summarize, let's recall that Mathieu was a valiant pioneer. In 1668, he was awarded letters of nobility. Unfor-tunately, they were invalid, wrote Father Archange Godbout, be-cause they had not been registered. The Intendant Jea
2 CONC n Talon wanted to amend this oversight by conceding at Villeneuve, on 3 November 1672, the seigneurie of Pointe-aux-Bouleaux, near Sainte-Croix de Lotbiniére.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mathieu and Marie raised a family of 16 children who have numerous descendants. Mathieu died on 18 December 1688 and was buried the next day at Québec. As for Marie Miville, she died at the Hôtel-Dieu on 5 September 1702, during h
2 CONC arvest time.
2 CONT
2 CONT =======================================================
