2 CONT
2 CONT Historiques Amyot et Laurier. Ancetre direct de Georges-Elie Amyot et de Sir Wilfrid Laurier.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Mathieu Amiot, Sieur de Villeneuve, born in France about 1628, owned a small lot in 1655, measuring 54 feet in frontage on the chemin Saint-Louis, to a depth of 18 feet, which ran as far as the south-western part of the land of Si
2 CONC eur Chartier de Lotbiniére, near Cap-aux-Diamants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On the occasion of his marriage to Marie Miville, on 21 November 1650, at Quebec, Mathieu received from his father-in-law Pierre Miville, a plot of land on the Grande-Allee, border-ing that of Jean Bourdon. Mathieu owned several o
2 CONC ther pieces: at Sillery, at the Chatellenie de Coulonges, at Saint-Augustin and at Cap-Rouge. The census of 1681 recorded the colonist Amiot as living in the seigneurie of Maure, between Tugal Catin and Jacques Lemarie, where he o
2 CONC wned three head of cattle and had thirty arpents of land under cultivation. 
2 CONT
2 CONT To summarize, let's recall that Mathieu was a valiant pioneer. In 1668, he was awarded letters of nobility. Unfor-tunately, they were invalid, wrote Father Archange Godbout, be-cause they had not been registered. The Intendant Jea
2 CONC n Talon wanted to amend this oversight by conceding at Villeneuve, on 3 November 1672, the seigneurie of Pointe-aux-Bouleaux, near Sainte-Croix de Lotbiniére. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mathieu and Marie raised a family of 16 children who have numerous descendants. Mathieu died on 18 December 1688 and was buried the next day at Québec. As for Marie Miville, she died at the Hôtel-Dieu on 5 September 1702, during h
2 CONC arvest time. 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT His father, who came originally from the neighbourhood of Soissons, was at Quebec from the summer of 1635 on. Mathieu, like his brother Jean, was for some years an interpreter for the Jesuits; he worked in their house at Trois-Riv
2 CONC ières and perhaps also in the Huron country. Then he became a settler, and during the remainder of his life he managed to accumulate a fairly sizable number of properties. Thus, in 1649, Governor Louis d’Ailleboust made him a gran
2 CONC t of land at Trois-Rivières; in addition, on the occasion of his marriage on 22 Oct. 1650, Marie Miville brought him as her dowry a property in the town of Quebec; in 1661 the Jesuits granted him a portion of land at Sillery, wher
2 CONC e he built a house for himself, whilst keeping his town residence; on 6 Sept. 1665 Jean Juchereau de Maur gave him an estate on Pointe Villeneuve, near Saint-Augustin de Portneuf, which he enlarged in 1677 and 1685; and on 3 Nov. 
2 CONC 1672 Talon granted him another domain, as a fief and seigneury, at Pointe aux Bouleaux.
2 CONT
2 CONT       As his possessions increased, Mathieu became a more and more important person in the colony. A notable at Quebec, he had taken part in the election of a syndic in 1664, and three years later the king acceded to Talon’s reque
2 CONC st to grant him letters of nobility. However, when these letters arrived in 1668 the intendant did not know whether he should have them registered in the Conseil Souverain of Quebec or in the Parlement of Paris. While awaiting the
2 CONC  reply from Versailles he learned that Louis XIV had abolished all titles not yet registered (1669). Three other settlers had received letters of nobility at the same time as Amiot. They or their descendants had them recognized de
2 CONC spite the 1669 ruling. But as Amiot apparently made no claim in respect of his, they were finally annulled.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Villeneuve left his heirs more debts and worries than assets. In 1703 the debts encumbering the estate still amounted to 700 livres, and Marie Miville, who had sold the lands for 1,500 livres, had died (September 1702), a vi
2 CONC ctim of the distress caused her by a lawsuit which her son Charles, the eldest of her 15 children, had brought against her.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Fils de Philippe et Anne Convent
2 CONT (CT 19 Audouart) avec Marie Miville
2 CONT
2 CONT Lettres de noblesse accordées en 1668, mais non enregistrées
2 CONT
2 CONT Concession de la seigneurie de la Pointe-aux-Bouleaux, près de Ste-Croix le 3-11-1672, augmentée le 16-4-1687 et vendue par sa veuve à Robert Choret le 5-4-1701
2 CONT
2 CONT Il "fut anobli par le roi et reçut en fief la pointe-Villeneuve à Sillery. C'est depuis, que le nom d'Amyot fut changé en celui de VILLENEUVE". 
2 CONT
2 CONT AMIOT (Amyot), dit Villeneuve, MATHIEU, donné, interprète, seigneur, né entre 1627 et 1629. probablement près de Chartres (Orléanais), de Philippe Amiot et d’Anne Convent, décédé à Québec le 18 novembre 1688.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Originaire des environs de Soissons, son père était à Québec dès l’été de 1635. Mathieu, comme son frère Jean, fut pendant quelques années interprète des Jésuites, travaillant chez eux à Trois-Rivières et peut-être aussi en 
2 CONC Huronie. Puis il se fit colon et réussit pendant le reste de sa vie à accumuler un nombre assez intéressant de possessions. Ainsi, en 1649, le gouverneur LouIs d’Ailleboust lui concéda une terre à Trois-Rivières ; en outre, lors d
2 CONC e son mariage, le 22 octobre 1650, Marie Miville lui apporta en dot une propriété dans la ville de Québec ; en 1661, les Jésuites lui firent concession d’un lot à Sillery, où il se construisit une maison, tout en conservant sa dem
2 CONC eure en ville ; le 6 septembre 1665, Jean Juchereau de Maur lui donna, sur la Pointe-Villeneuve, près de Saint-Augustin de Portneuf, une propriété qu’il agrandit en 1677 et en 1685 ; le 3 novembre 1672, Talon lui avait concédé en 
2 CONC fief et seigneurie un autre domaine à la Pointe-aux-Bouleaux.
2 CONT
2 CONT       À mesure que ses biens s’accumulaient, Mathieu devenait un personnage de plus en plus important dans la colonie. Comme notable, à Québec, il avait participé à l’élection d’un syndic en 1664 et, trois ans plus tard, le roi ag
2 CONC réa la requête de Talon de lui octroyer des lettres de noblesse. Cependant, quand elles arrivèrent, en 1668, l’intendant ignorait s’il devait les faire enregistrer au Conseil souverain de Québec ou au parlement de Paris. En attend
2 CONC ant la réponse de Versailles, il apprit que Louis XIV avait aboli tous les titres non encore enregistrés (1669). Trois autres colons avaient reçu des lettres de noblesse en même temps qu’Amiot. Eux ou leurs descendants les firent 
2 CONC reconnaître en dépit de l’ordonnance de 1669. Amiot n’ayant fait aucune revendication au sujet des siennes, semble-t-il, elles furent définitivement annulées.
2 CONT
2 CONT       Villeneuve laissa à ses héritiers plus de dettes et de soucis que de biens. En 1703, les dettes de la succession s’élevaient encore à 700ª et Marie Miville, qui avait vendu les terres pour 1 500ª, était morte (septembre 1702
2 CONC ) des angoisses que lui causait un procès intenté contre elle par son fils Charles, l’aîné de ses 15 enfants. Source: Dictionnaire biographique de Canada 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT AMIOT (Amyot), said Villeneuve, MATHIEU, given, interpreter, Lord, born between 1627 and 1629. probably near Chartres (Orléans), Philippe Amiot and Anne Convent, died in Quebec City on November 18, 1688.Originally from the vicinit
2 CONC y of Soissons, his father was in Quebec City as early as the summer of 1635. Mathieu, like his brother John, was for some years the Jesuits performer, working at home in Trois-Rivières and perhaps also in Huronia. Then he became c
2 CONC olon and succeeded during the rest of his life to accumulate a fairly decent number of possessions. Thus, in 1649, Governor LouIs d'Ailleboust him conceded land in Trois-Rivières; In addition, during his marriage, October 22, 1650
2 CONC , Marie McCarty brought in dowry property in Quebec City; In 1661, the Jesuits made him grant of a lot at Sillery, where he built a House, while retaining his residence in the city. September 6, 1665, Jean Juchereau de Maur gave h
2 CONC im on the tip-Villeneuve, near Saint-Augustin de Portneuf, a property that it enlarges in 1677 and in 1685; November 3, 1672, heel he conceded in fief and Lordship another domain in the Pointe - aux-bouleaux.That its goods were pi
2 CONC ling up, Mathieu became a more important figure in the colony. As notable, in Quebec City, he had participated in the election of a trustee in 1664, and three years later, the King accepted the request of heel to grant letters of 
2 CONC nobility. However, when they arrived, in 1668, the intendant was unaware whether he should to register to the Conseil souverain of Quebec or the Parliament of Paris. Pending the response of Versailles, he learned that Louis XIV ha
2 CONC d abolished all titles not yet registered (1669). Three other settlers had received letters of nobility at the same time as Amiot. Them or their descendants made them recognize despite the order of 1669. Amiot having no claim abou
2 CONC t his own, seems, they were definitely cancelled.Villeneuve left his heirs more debts and worries property. In 1703, the debts of the estate still amounted to 700ª and Marie McCarty, who had sold the land for 1 livres, was dead (S
2 CONC eptember 1702) of anguish that caused him a lawsuit brought against it by his son Charles, the eldest of his 15 children.Source: Dictionary of Canada Biography
2 CONT --------------------
